Section 3.1: Breaking Changes Definition
A change is considered BREAKING (Major version bump required) if:
(a) Any function parameter is removed or its type is narrowed (e.g., uint32 to uint16) such that valid inputs for the old version might fail or truncate in the new version.
(b) The return type is NARROWED (e.g., int32 to int16) such that values previously returnable by the old function cannot be represented in the new type without overflow or loss of precision.
(c) A new required parameter is added without a default value mechanism compatible with C linkage.

Section 3.2: Non-Breaking Exceptions
A change is NON-BREAKING (Minor/Patch) if:
(a) New optional parameters are added at the END of the argument list, provided the ABI allows callers to ignore them (Note: In strict C environments, adding args is BREAKING unless variadic or macro-wrapped; HOWEVER, see Rule 3.2.b).
(b) Rule 3.2.b (The 'Strict Return' Override): Even if parameters are added safely, if the Return Type is NARROWED (Rule 3.1.b), the change is IMMEDIATELY classified as BREAKING regardless of parameter safety. The return type constraint takes precedence over parameter extensibility.
(c) Return type is WIDENED (e.g., int16 to int32).

Section 3.3: Precedence
When rules conflict, the rule resulting in the most restrictive classification (Breaking) applies, unless a specific 'Safe Harbor' clause is invoked. No Safe Harbor exists for return type narrowing in real-time control paths.

